WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.589 --> 00:00:24.310
So that makes two wonderful evenings of
conversation

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:28.720
in one evening.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.390
How many people were able to park without
difficulty coming here tonight?

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:41.390
And how many committed a violation in the
way they parked?

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.290
Let's start with of questions for Donald who

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.120
I think has some

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.860
very clear

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:51.800
and challenging

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.790
preachments for

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.140
the way we manage

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.310
pavement and cars in

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.170
many places including

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:01.279
Yale

00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:02.300
and New Haven

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:09.300
and the state of Connecticut.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.260
Tell us more how we can get rid of

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:17.930
on-site parking requirements.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:20.970
This is complicated.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.100
Well,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.440
I was thinking as I was listening

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:27.550
to Tom, 

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:34.440
it seemed to be one of the themes of what he said
was that driving seemed simple but was very complicated.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.220
My view is that parking seems complicated
but is very simple.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:44.670
And it would be much simpler if there
were no off-street parking requirements.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.889
And I think

00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:47.159
one of the 

00:01:47.159 --> 00:01:51.350
reasons I think that it can happen is that
some cities have already done it,

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:54.170
and a comparison I like to make

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:56.990
is between San Francisco and Los Angeles.

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:02.880
Some of you have heard of Disney Hall, the new concert
hall by Frank Gehry in

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:06.299
Los Angeles. Los Angeles requires as a minimum

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:12.339
fifty times more parking than San Francisco allows
as the maximum.

00:02:12.339 --> 00:02:16.319
That in Los angeles we have a minimum with
no maximum, and 

00:02:16.319 --> 00:02:19.099
San Francisco has a maximum with no minimum.

00:02:19.099 --> 00:02:21.439
Now if this were a real science,

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:25.419
wouldn't you ask what's going on?

00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:31.239
If in one city the treatment
for tuberculosis was blood transfusion and

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:34.230
the city it was blood letting,

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:39.229
wouldn't any scientists say that there has
to be some mistake here?

00:02:39.229 --> 00:02:40.250
But we have

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.909
most of the cities that you really want to
go to

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:50.999
like Boston and New York and Chicago and San Francisco
and London and Paris and Copenhagen,

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:53.659
they all have maximums with no minimums.

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:56.659
The cities that you don't want to go to

00:02:56.659 --> 00:02:59.949
like Phoenix and Houston and Los Angeles,

00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:03.699
they have minimums with no maximums,

00:03:03.699 --> 00:03:08.849
so I think you have to decide what kind of
city you want to be more like, and

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:14.369
I think that we've had a long history
of trying to force up the parking supply

00:03:14.369 --> 00:03:16.590
and not allowing anything that doesn't have

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.389
what we say is the

00:03:19.389 --> 00:03:21.459
minimum.

00:03:21.459 --> 00:03:25.879
I think you could just point to cities that you
would rather go to, and I think one of the cities

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:27.390
that I really like is

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:32.349
one of the best places on earth for pedestrians,
is Carmel, California

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.390
that doesn't allow any off-street in the
central business district.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.919
They don't want any cars driving across sidewalks.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:45.229
They want all the interior space used for shops
and art galleries. They think

00:03:45.229 --> 00:03:47.439
there's more to be gained for the city

00:03:47.439 --> 00:03:54.439
to have people park at the periphery and walk in. It's one of 
the best places on Earth to be a pedestrian.

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:00.029
I'm not saying it's easy to do, but I think
that...

00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:02.029
or I believe in the

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:05.400
Alice in Wonderland, once you say something
three times that it's true,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.809
and I just have to keep saying it

00:04:07.809 --> 00:04:08.820
over and over and over.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:12.759
And I think more cities are trying it.

00:04:15.939 --> 00:04:17.349
Other questions?

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:19.160
Don, I'm curious: there have been a few municipalities

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.309
mainly in the west

00:04:21.309 --> 00:04:23.710
that have changed their parking policies

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.509
since your book came out,

00:04:25.509 --> 00:04:27.499
and I'm wondering whether

00:04:27.499 --> 00:04:30.120
there is much opposition

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.789
from the point of view of either

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:36.199
it chases potential customers away from business
areas, or that it costs too much

00:04:37.229 --> 00:04:39.059
for people

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:43.139
who have very modest means.

00:04:43.139 --> 00:04:49.740
Well, naturally I hear that question a lot.
And I've become, I hope,

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.059
practiced in answering it

00:04:51.059 --> 00:04:53.620
because I do have

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:56.410
a second life of going to cities, giving my spiel,

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:03.180
and there's always somebody who gets up and it isn't a
question, they say, "I'll never

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.520
come back to downtown Santa Rosa if you

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:09.699
run the meters after 6 pm,"

00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:13.499
and the argument I would make to the merchants

00:05:13.499 --> 00:05:17.160
and the property owners--they seem very receptive--
is that

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:22.649
who do you think will spend more in a store or
leave a better tip at a restaurant?

00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:26.469
Somebody who will come only if they can park
free after they drive around long enough

00:05:26.469 --> 00:05:28.039
to find a space

00:05:28.039 --> 00:05:30.110
or someone is willing to

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:34.399
pay for parking if they can easily
find a space?

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:37.180
Which will make the area

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:41.760
more prosperous? Say in old Pasadena when they put in
parking meters, the merchants thought

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.039
we're going to drive everybody away

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:49.849
once the city told the merchants and property owners you'll
get the revenue,  

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.959
like that they said that's different, let's run the

00:05:51.959 --> 00:05:53.509
meters until midnight. Let's run them on Sunday.

00:05:53.509 --> 00:05:58.580
It's what you do with the money that is
very key. And if I really don't like

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.569
the person who asked the question, I say

00:06:00.569 --> 00:06:04.599
if you aren't willing to pay for parking
downtown maybe you'll find you'll get a better

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:08.460
deal at the food court at a suburban mall with
ample free parking.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:12.720
So everybody will win.

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:19.120
Let's get a question out of the front two rows. 

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:21.869
Don, I'd like to ask you

00:06:21.869 --> 00:06:28.610
what your opinion is about parking supply
at rail stations,

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.559
and how you

00:06:30.559 --> 00:06:32.949
address the

00:06:32.949 --> 00:06:35.959
supposed

00:06:35.959 --> 00:06:40.759
demand issue there.

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:43.509
The local case may be

00:06:43.509 --> 00:06:46.889
illustrative.

00:06:46.889 --> 00:06:52.650
Well, you could probably predict what I'm going to say, and that
is to charge the right price for parking.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:58.309
If all the spaces are full, the price should go up.

00:06:58.309 --> 00:07:03.049
They're doing this in the San Francisco area, where
the BART system, the

00:07:03.049 --> 00:07:08.409
Bay Area Rapid Transit system was supposed to
lead to wonderful development around

00:07:08.409 --> 00:07:13.249
the stations, but instead there were just parking lots, 
and they were 

00:07:13.249 --> 00:07:14.680
overflowing with cars.

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:16.200
I'd say there's something

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.539
different that I would say as well, it's that

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:22.119
not only charging the right price in the parking lots,

00:07:22.119 --> 00:07:23.710
but I would say that

00:07:23.710 --> 00:07:26.349
I haven't seen this happen really,

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:29.460
is that if there are permanent parking districts

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.350
around the station so that you can't park on the curb
on the street without

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:35.839
paying for it,

00:07:35.839 --> 00:07:40.900
that you should allow the commuters 
to pay for parking by the day.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:44.860
But they would have to pay to park on the street,
and you would set the price so that

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:45.649
there are only

00:07:45.649 --> 00:07:46.560
three or four

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.749
commuter cars on every block

00:07:49.749 --> 00:07:52.739
and spend that money to improve the

00:07:52.739 --> 00:07:56.840
neighborhood so that the parkers
and the residents

00:07:56.840 --> 00:07:59.500
will be more symbiotic.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.509
That the people who want to drive to the train
station

00:08:03.509 --> 00:08:05.399
will be paying guests.

00:08:05.399 --> 00:08:10.569
They won't be freeloaders anymore; they'll be paying
guests, and that's very different.

00:08:10.569 --> 00:08:15.650
Many universities do this…well, not many, but some, like 

00:08:15.650 --> 00:08:18.349
the University of Colorado at Boulder, which does everything
right it seems…environmental things.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.979
In all the surrounding neighborhoods they

00:08:22.979 --> 00:08:26.379
do surveys to see if there are some

00:08:26.379 --> 00:08:30.189
empty spaces during the day because almost
everybody has to work during the day

00:08:30.189 --> 00:08:31.419
to make a living,

00:08:31.419 --> 00:08:33.340
pay their mortgages in Boulder,

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.430
so that if there's less than

00:08:35.430 --> 00:08:39.120
75% occupancy, they'll sell up to

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:39.649
four non-resident

00:08:39.649 --> 00:08:42.820
daytime permits to

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:47.990
outsiders, and they charge them every month what
the residents have to pay for a year.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:52.040
And the money is used to reduce the cost of the
permits for the residents. So I think we have

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:56.310
some of the most valuable land on earth now being
given away free to cars, and the more

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.250
you can figure out a way

00:08:57.250 --> 00:08:58.620
make that land 

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:01.330
pay for public services,

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:04.660
the better off we'll be.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:07.310
In the back.

00:09:07.310 --> 00:09:14.310
A number of the things you mentioned...

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:37.420
Well, as I understand it

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:42.360
if we start charging higher prices for parking
in the business district, will this

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.830
cause some people to park in the nearby neighborhoods?

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:57.790
Well,

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:02.650
most cities would give the residents a
permit to park at the meter

00:10:02.650 --> 00:10:03.530
without paying.

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:08.470
That the residents usually get preference
in any district because they have to

00:10:08.470 --> 00:10:09.920
be

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.460
bought off.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:16.700
So I think to make it politically popular,
everybody who has as say, the residents,

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.400
the merchants and the property wonders, they all have
to be satisfied I think. And the way to satisfy

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:21.930
the residents

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.690
is to give them permit parking.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:30.010
And that in these permit parking districts, if 
there are some vacant spaces, you should sell the excess

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:35.180
to outsiders and use the money to improve the neighborhood.

00:10:35.180 --> 00:10:38.010
And this is beginning to happen in cities, that they...
 
0:10:38.010,0:10:40.000
Here is some valuable land;

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:44.670
let's make use of it. The employees in stores can park
there during

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.130
the day,

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:50.600
and they'll pay for it or their employer will pay for it.

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:51.750
Let's pursue that

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.520
a little further, Don. Let's suppose we...

00:10:55.520 --> 00:10:57.740
the city,

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.990
always in need of cash,

00:10:59.990 --> 00:11:01.750
looks at all the 

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:06.330
metered space or or meterable space on its streets,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.070
and says let's have an auction.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:11.170
Investors from everywhere,

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.470
hedge funds,

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:15.810
investment banks,

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:16.790
whomsoever,

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:21.140
we're going to sell all these street parking spaces,

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:24.390
and you can price it anyway you want.

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.070
Would we be prepared to make

00:11:26.070 --> 00:11:29.290
that leap?

00:11:29.290 --> 00:11:34.620
Well I wouldn't recommend it. Chicago
did do it, and the reason that they gave

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:40.130
was that they did not have the political
will to charge the

00:11:40.130 --> 00:11:42.330
market rate prices for parking.

00:11:42.330 --> 00:11:45.470
And they outsourced the need for political
will

00:11:45.470 --> 00:11:47.510
to Morgan Stanley...

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.730
made over a billion dollars for

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.660
the media revenue for 75 years.

00:11:55.270 --> 00:11:57.250
And it's a

00:11:57.250 --> 00:12:01.720
perfect example-- I'm sure that would happen to any city.
They said we'll put this money into a lock

00:12:01.720 --> 00:12:03.700
box, and we'll provide you know

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:07.420
revenue for schools or whatever for the next 75 years.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:10.130
Within two years all the money was gone.

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:16.610
That's what we're doing everywhere is selling
off assets to pay for current expenses.

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:20.890
That's a bad idea for a household. It's a bad
idea for city.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:24.410
If a city cannot get its act together and

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:28.950
manage its parking properly, maybe it should lease it

00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:35.020
to a private operator. Maybe it should have
a revenue sharing agreement where they split the

00:12:35.020 --> 00:12:36.990
revenue or something like that.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:38.950
But I think its only if the city

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:44.400
says it can't do it,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.080
that it doesn't have the capacity to do it,
then it should look to the outside

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:49.649
operators, but

00:12:49.649 --> 00:12:54.260
they can be brought in as partners rather
than selling it all.

00:12:54.260 --> 00:13:00.660
We're going to turn to Tom Vanderbilt in just a second,
but I thought it might be…Sure.

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:04.340
Don, in the slide I had from Perdue University, 
one thing I forgot to

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:06.079
mention was that Pijanowsky found that

00:13:06.079 --> 00:13:08.000
it's interesting

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.930
despite this abundance of parking, he found that

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:14.770
surveys still found that there was not
enough parking,

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:18.070
and I don't know if this is necessarily a peak demand
issue or...

00:13:18.070 --> 00:13:20.330
It leads to the question or sort of the psychological

00:13:20.330 --> 00:13:21.899
perception of what

00:13:21.899 --> 00:13:25.890
is available parking and how that sort of plays,
and I was wondering if this has been

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:30.410
studied and to what extent and...

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:35.990
Yes it has. And parking is so much simpler than driving
that it's been studied very well

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:37.380
that whenever

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:38.710
there's a

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:43.140
place like West Hollywood, there's a hot
spot

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:46.470
in Los Angeles now, very trendy,

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:51.280
there's a terrible shortage of parking, including
off-street parking. We need more of it.

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:55.819
And I went into the parking garage, and you take
pictures of the ground floor spaces,

00:13:55.819 --> 00:13:58.570
and they're just covered with oil spots.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.470
You go up to the top deck,

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:04.280
and it looks as though there's never been a car
on it.

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:06.070
That's pristine, white

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:10.670
concrete, as though there's never been an oil spot.
I went to the top of the Temple Street garage.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:14.050
I did not see a single oil spot.

00:14:14.050 --> 00:14:15.279
I think that

00:14:15.279 --> 00:14:17.660
the spaces that you can see

00:14:17.660 --> 00:14:21.210
are the first spaces to be taken, and it
looks as though

00:14:21.939 --> 00:14:26.420
there's a shortage, but when you go
to the spaces you can't see--there are a lot of them--

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:32.290
but getting back to my one note, I would say
the first spaces you see are the curb spaces.

00:14:32.290 --> 00:14:35.970
And if they're all full, you think there's no parking.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:39.180
And if we've got the price right for curb parking,

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:44.300
nobody could say there's a shortage of parking because
everywhere they went they would see one or two vacant spaces

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:48.700
on every block. They could never say there's
a shortage because…

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:50.660
well, some people would I suppose, 

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:55.010
but most people wouldn't.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.010
This is really

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.960
potent and interesting stop and persuasive
to me.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:06.830
And ultimately there's a distributive side of this
where I mean people with

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:09.500
high need to park and low incomes

00:15:09.500 --> 00:15:13.560
are going to get squeezed by the market mechanism
in this case as in

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.000
many other.

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:17.710
Are we prepared to

00:15:17.710 --> 00:15:20.890
override that consideration, Don?

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:25.260
Well, I think I have a good answer for that as well
because it's obviously the first thing

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:26.570
that people say,

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:29.250
And I think I'm not accusing you of saying it,

00:15:29.250 --> 00:15:31.730
but the people who don't want to pay for parking

00:15:31.730 --> 00:15:35.860
they push poor people out in front of them
like human shields,

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:41.100
saying don't charge for parking, it'll hurt poor
people when they really mean don't charge for

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:43.710
parking because it will hurt me.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:44.899
I could say that

00:15:44.899 --> 00:15:46.369
some of the people who would

00:15:46.369 --> 00:15:49.680
benefit most are the poorest

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:55.390
part of any population. New Haven has lots of
poor people. They could benefit from higher

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:56.439
public revenues.

00:15:56.439 --> 00:16:01.850
Buses would run faster because
there wouldn't be so many cars

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:05.650
driving around, hunting for a free curb
parking space

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:10.680
and slowing down the buses. That's one of the
main reasons that San Francisco's doing it. They

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:15.760
justify it by it'll help poor people more than
anybody else.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:20.340
Really poor people.

00:16:20.340 --> 00:16:23.089
Okay, we've got a little time left and

00:16:23.089 --> 00:16:24.010
another

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:26.290
fascinating argument.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.230
Let's see what we can do with

00:16:28.230 --> 00:16:33.730
questions directed to Tom Vanderbilt.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:35.520
Thank you.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:40.580
I want to take advantage of the fact that Tom
Vanderbilt is a journalist and there are other journalists

00:16:40.580 --> 00:16:41.560
in the crowd.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:47.200
I've been on a personal tear for some
time about the way we have refer to transit

00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:54.200
as public transit or mass transit or public
transportation or mass transportation.

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:56.480
Nothing or hardly ever does a good

00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:02.300
thing come after the word mass or after the word

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:03.459
public. So think murder and toilets

00:17:03.459 --> 00:17:05.429
for example.

00:17:05.429 --> 00:17:07.600
As a journalist I would love to 

00:17:07.600 --> 00:17:09.160
beg you to

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.629
only ever refer to transit as transit

00:17:11.629 --> 00:17:16.430
ever. Thank you.

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:20.089
Good. Editorial acknowledged.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.920
Questions out there. Yes, back right.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:24.949
You'll have to bellow it out.

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:39.750
That would be a more

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.510
hopeful sign. Often, you know,

00:17:41.510 --> 00:17:45.020
kids these days aren't really playing around
these signs because, you know, they're inside on their

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:46.840
Nintendos or whatever, but yeah.

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:50.750
Schools zones are a strange thing to
me. There's been some studies that

00:17:50.750 --> 00:17:55.540
actually stop people after they've driven through a school
zone, they've tracked them on radar,

00:17:55.540 --> 00:17:57.379
wondering how fast they were going, then asked them

00:17:57.379 --> 00:17:58.610
how fast were you going.

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:00.820
They're off by ten miles an hour.

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:01.720
And this is

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.390
why I have a problem with certain…

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:06.730
in Texas for example there's new legislation, which
bans...

00:18:06.730 --> 00:18:10.180
you can…how does it work in Texas…it now bans

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:15.659
hand held cellphone use in school zone areas,
but it's okay to use your hand held cellphone

00:18:15.659 --> 00:18:16.610
everywhere else,

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:19.900
so this sort of assumes that number one you've seen the sign and

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.680
number two you're actually going to stop your conversation
right at the moment you enter this, so...

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:27.130
And it begs the question are you asking
people to drive in one manner here and then

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:29.669
back to less safely in other areas, so...

00:18:29.669 --> 00:18:34.760
And clearly there's a reason some schools
need amplified school zone protection

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:38.200
because of the habits of children, but

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.080
yeah…This is the argument to go
back to the question about seeing things is 

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:44.550
actually so much more potent than seeing a sign advising

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.860
of them, and the famous argument here is

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:50.680
Brian Arthur's safety

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:53.790
in numbers argument, which is basically that

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:55.200
the more you see of something,

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.450
the more likely you are to actually see that thing,

00:18:57.450 --> 00:18:58.920
which has been

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:02.500
advanced to help explain why countries like
the Netherlands and

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:04.429
Denmark in addition to having better facilities
to begin with

00:19:04.429 --> 00:19:06.960
have such lower

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:10.200
pedestrian and cyclist injury rates.

00:19:10.200 --> 00:19:13.560
Drivers just are so accustomed to dealing with
and seeing them,

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.890
and it's sort of a nice

00:19:15.890 --> 00:19:21.270
cycle: the more pedestrians you see, you sort of drive
slower and you see the pedestrians for a longer time.

00:19:28.590 --> 00:19:32.690
Tom, I share your affinity for

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:34.800
incentive-based programs

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:35.680
and for

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.040
Thayer's approach

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.740
nudge and something like that, and I'm curious
if you've come across

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:42.890
any incentive-based programs

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:49.890
that you see that are actually working to impact certain 
traffic patterns or negative traffic patterns that you've seen in the past.

00:19:54.690 --> 00:19:58.270
Well, the obvious thing here would be congestion

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:01.650
pricing, which is a disincentive of course. but...

00:20:01.650 --> 00:20:05.790
I'm trying to think of some of the things I've seen in the U.S.
has only been sort of hypothetical,

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:09.650
giving people for example… a program called

00:20:09.650 --> 00:20:12.180
Choices…

00:20:12.180 --> 00:20:16.200
done in the Puget Sound, Seattle area, which
gave people sort of an account.

00:20:16.200 --> 00:20:18.660
an imaginary account of money that they could

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:22.510
based on the amount of miles they drove at the end of the
month they'd be given

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:24.120
some of that money back

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:26.140
based on if they drove at a different time,

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:28.940
off-peak, or if they just drove less in general.

00:20:28.940 --> 00:20:29.860
And that program had

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:31.570
you know, pretty robust results.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:32.660
People wanted

00:20:32.660 --> 00:20:34.560
money back. And that was sort of hypothetical and it was

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:37.419
a congestion pricing lead-in

00:20:37.419 --> 00:20:41.310
that never went anywhere, but when you...

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:47.230
You've got me for other...

00:20:47.230 --> 00:20:51.100
Just a quick thing. I'm not saying that California
does anything right, but

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:56.620
one thing that I would recommend is called parking
cash out is that many employers give

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:59.059
their employees free parking

00:20:59.059 --> 00:21:00.619
in California,

00:21:00.619 --> 00:21:05.450
that if you have over fifty employees, and if you rent the
parking spaces to your employees, you have

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:10.190
to offer the employees the option to take
the cash value of the parking if they don't take

00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:11.110
the parking.

00:21:11.110 --> 00:21:13.080
It's not charging them for parking,

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:16.630
it just makes them aware they have an
alternative. They'd rather

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.500
ride the bus, ride transit

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.169
or if they'd rather bicycle or walk or carpool,
they could have the cash value of the parking.

00:21:23.169 --> 00:21:27.519
The employer can't say I
will give you free parking or nothing,

00:21:27.519 --> 00:21:32.700
which is what almost every employer the United
States does.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.010
I think we have time for one or two

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:36.940
concluding questions. Yes.

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:13.700
Yeah, good observations. On the first one,
it's not a new observation that

00:22:13.700 --> 00:22:17.400
people feel insulated from the environment
around them as they're driving a car,

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.400
even with the windows being up this
is something where even

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:23.919
cabin noise has been studied, and the absence of 

00:22:23.919 --> 00:22:24.620
cabin noise,

00:22:24.620 --> 00:22:26.390
people actually drive faster.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:45.770
On the second comment, it reminds me of
Safe Routes to School did a study of 

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:50.679
traffic injuries for children around schools,
and the majority of kids were injured by

00:22:50.679 --> 00:22:52.169
other parents taking their

00:22:52.169 --> 00:22:54.630
kids to that same school.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.690
And this is something again

00:22:56.690 --> 00:23:00.530
comparing the early seventies to now, when I was
young, growing up in the suburbs

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:02.250
yet like the Bradys, I walked to school. This was not a

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.300
bizarre behavior...

00:23:16.640 --> 00:23:18.350
Last question, George Edwards in the back.

00:24:41.810 --> 00:24:43.560
The arrangement to which

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:48.800
the speaker referred is that the city
in 1991 leased

00:24:48.800 --> 00:24:52.880
High Street and Wall Street the Yale for
99 years.

00:24:56.270 --> 00:24:58.490
And the object was

00:24:58.490 --> 00:25:01.180
to put cash in the city and

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:06.600
give the university planning control over
its interior streets.

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:07.409
And George,

00:25:07.409 --> 00:25:10.419
I guess your objection is

00:25:10.419 --> 00:25:13.320
what goes into the meters

00:25:13.320 --> 00:25:15.490
does go to the city,

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:18.149
but the planning control is with Yale, and 

00:25:18.149 --> 00:25:25.149
I believe that you're a long-standing
opponent of that arrangement.

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:27.890
Okay,

00:25:27.890 --> 00:25:32.110
we have a distinguished member of the audience
here from

00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.890
Connecticut DOT and he's got a last question.

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:39.049
It's not so much a question as really an observation.
I'm John Murray with the Connecticut DOT.

00:25:39.049 --> 00:25:43.249
Yes, we are going to expand parking capacity here
in New Haven as part of a larger vision

00:25:43.249 --> 00:25:45.010
for transit-oriented development

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:47.460
around the station,

00:25:47.460 --> 00:25:51.490
and given the current state of revenues here in Connecticut,
we are going to charge market rate for that

00:25:51.490 --> 00:25:54.620
parking, and I can assure you of
all of that.

00:25:54.620 --> 00:25:58.710
You mentioned Phoenix earlier today. Very
good observation. They actually recently built

00:25:58.710 --> 00:26:00.020
a light rail system

00:26:00.020 --> 00:26:04.740
in Phoenix, connecting Mesa through Tempe, to downtown Phoenix,

00:26:04.740 --> 00:26:08.620
expected 17,000 riders per day on it. I worked on the system
and actually oversaw the

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:09.900
opening of the system.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.139
I'm sorry.

00:26:11.139 --> 00:26:16.089
And now the ridership
on that system despite free, ample parking

00:26:16.089 --> 00:26:18.509
in downtown is 40,000 people per day.

00:26:18.509 --> 00:26:22.490
The system just opened up in December 2008,
so as long as there's a good

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:22.960
alternative

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:25.490
to parking downtown, and that may be

00:26:25.490 --> 00:26:27.990
public transportation, 

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:31.409
there's ways in which we can achieve this. And lastly

00:26:31.409 --> 00:26:34.360
the behavior that you talked about on
riders,

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:37.720
I wish I had been able to sort of document
this all the time,

00:26:37.720 --> 00:26:43.679
and I hope my wife is not seeing this by
any chance, but she…We grew up in Boston, and she

00:26:43.679 --> 00:26:47.240
didn't get her a driver's license until she was 32
years old because we moved from Boston. We actually

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:48.660
had to drive cars,

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:50.049
and she went from being a raving lunatic

00:26:52.380 --> 00:26:56.380
and quite honestly I really
hope she's not watching this. She's a lawyer. 

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:58.350
It'd be very bad for me.

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:03.820
But her behavior since we moved back
to a more transit-friendly Connecticut

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:07.960
is much better, and she's in a much better mood
for all that, so.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:10.290
Thanks, and let's have a round of applause for these
wonderful speakers.

